  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1248 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nFog has developed over the Missouri River and areas just to the east \nduring the early nighttime hours. This fog will be spreading east \nthrough the night into Saturday morning. A dense fog advisory has \nbeen issued into Saturday morning as visibilities could drop below 1 \nmile at times in areas with the fog. Additionally, some areas that \nexperience the fog will have temperatures below freezing, which \ncould cause some freezing fog and localized areas of potentially \nslick conditions. Southwestern winds west of the Missouri River will \nkeep the fog from spreading west. These southwest winds will spread \neast through the night and will help erode the edge of the fog as it \nmoves east into the morning. \n\nThrough the morning into the afternoon, the winds will become more \nwesterly and will start to speed up. Wind gusts over most of central \nand northeastern SD will be between 20-25 mph, with stronger wind \ngusts up to 30 mph over Corson and Dewey counties and the eastern \nedge of the Sisseton Hills north of HWY 12. Minimum relative \nhumidity values will be between 40-50% over Corson and Dewey \ncounties, and with the stronger winds and dry vegetation, the lower \nhumidity values will cause high Grassland Fire Danger Index values. \nThe higher humidity values and slightly lower wind speeds will keep \nthe Grassland Fire Danger Index values in moderate category for the \nrest of central and northeastern SD. \n\nWarmer air will be coming on the southerly and westerly winds today \nthrough Monday. This will warm temperatures to be about 10-20 \ndegrees warmer than normal for this time of the year, with highs in \nthe 50s to low 60s. Models show a frontal boundary move through on \nMonday, which will turn the winds and start bringing in some colder \nair. This cooler air won't cool things too much, but will cool \ntemperatures over the following days to be around normal. While the \nfront will move cold air in and turn winds to be from the northwest, \nthe lack of moisture in the atmosphere will help to keep \nprecipitation from developing. The next chance for some isolated \nrain showers looks to be Tuesday evening into Wednesday, though it \nis only about 10-20% chance over northeastern SD. This is because \nthe models vary if anything does develop and the timing of it. \nModels will likely change what will happen, so an eye will need to \nbe kept on the models if they change or come to an agreement.",
